A||N||22||Monkeyman||Driving lights as headlights||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||11-24-2001||11:58 AM||||I'm considering installing driving lights in the front fascia to use as headlights (working on a non pop up headlight deal).  Any suggestions?  Would driving lights be blinding to oncoming traffic?  Would it actually be better to use fog lights (real ones, not just driving lights with amber lenses) around town?  Any good sources for lights?  (I've already checked out JCWhitney.)<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/patriot.gif">    <BR><B><FONT COLOR="red">Proud</FONT> <FONT COLOR="white">to be an</FONT> <FONT COLOR="blue">American</FONT>!!</B>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Haagster||11-24-2001||12:13 PM||||Would driving lights in the facia produce enough illumination to "legally" replace the headlights?<P>Just a thought<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_8.jpg">    <BR>87GT Auto<p>[This message has been edited by Haagster (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||avengador1||11-24-2001||12:31 PM||||Someone can correct me if I'm wrong, but I believe that using driving lights as headlights would be illegal. I think there is a height regulation that has to be meet and a wattage regulation.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||11-24-2001||12:53 PM||||I think driving lights will produce enough illumination.  I only drive around town and rarely at night.  I just want to be able to remove my stock headlights and play around with them for a while.  I obviously can't do that if I need them for driving.  I'm not worried about legalities.  I can deal with that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||11-24-2001||02:37 PM||||There is a height requirement and other laws that won't let you use driving lights as headlights. The classify them as "auxilary lights" That is why they had to start changing factory driving lights so they only come on when the headlights are on. They use to have it where you could turn the driving lights on with parking lights.<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||BlackStang95||11-24-2001||03:20 PM||||If you're not worried about a minor ticket or two go for it...but you will likely get them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Reality||11-24-2001||04:00 PM||||Not sure if it helps any, but I know in some "cities" it is actually an unwritten rule that you drive in a downtown setting with you headlights off. Mostly this pertains to a well lit area. The headlights are considered blinding so everyone just drives with aux lights and parking lights. This includes city operated vehicles. Just my input.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/realitysignature_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||11-24-2001||04:16 PM||||Dave--True, but I was thinking about Lothars car.  I wonder how he gets by with it.  (Or does he just drive it to shows?)  I can't find any decent lights anyway so I think I'll just work on a non pop up light.  I'll probably end up using driving lights (I can't afford any of the "kits" right now).  I'm not worried about any tickets or inspections (we don't have any here).  As long as it looks good and works well (safety first), I'll be satisfied.<P>New question.  Who's made their own non pop ups?  URLs, etc?  The only one I've seen so far is Martin Whites.||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||11-24-2001||05:44 PM||||I would STRONGLY advise against this!<P>There are DOT approved high and low beam headlamp assemblies that can be adapted to your application easially and will look great.<P>There is NO need to do anything so dangerous as to remove headlights from your car (even if you replace them with 'driving lights' they will be ALL wrong, dim, and the WRONG pattern.)<P>In a word, NO!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||11-24-2001||06:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>Dave--True, but I was thinking about Lothars car.  I wonder how he gets by with it.  (Or does he just drive it to shows?)  I can't find any decent lights anyway so I think I'll just work on a non pop up light.  I'll probably end up using driving lights (I can't afford any of the "kits" right now).  I'm not worried about any tickets or inspections (we don't have any here).  As long as it looks good and works well (safety first), I'll be satisfied.<P>New question.  Who's made their own non pop ups?  URLs, etc?  The only one I've seen so far is Martin Whites.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lothar's only goes to shows. I've never seen him drive it anywhere else. In fact there are very few shows he actually goes to. This year was the first time I've seen him at a Fierorama. I would guess the only reason he came was because the TV show people were there.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||11-24-2001||06:12 PM||||I have driving lites in my reg lite position 1 set for lower beam, 1 set for higher beam. They throw much more lite than Fiero OEM. If I really wanna see, I have extra driving lites below the bumper. There prob illegal like that in most states. But Ive done it since 1976 in various vehicles and never been stopped. A little side note, most of the newer cars have plastic lenses and just a reflective plastic coating inside. After a few years the lenses get dull and scratched and the coating peels off from moisture. I had a late model Lincoln Mark in here with headlites so dim they looked like marker lites and it was legal, all the silver coating was gone inside. Another one that comes to mind are Escorts, they look like parking lites after 10 years. Id say go for it, all they can do is make you put them back. If you want some Dot approved lites that are same size of fog lites, get sealed beams from 88-89 Toronado. I have a set of 4 with buckets I was going to use.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||11-24-2001||06:46 PM||||rogergarrison--Email or PM me, please.  I'm interested in your flush mounted headlight conversion.  As a matter of fact, I just spent 2 hours doing a search for just that.  I came up short.  One lonely thread had a link to some pics of yours but it was dead.  I might be interested in taking the Toronado headlights off your hands, too if you want to sell them.||||reg||1
